## Why This Is Asked
Tests whether the candidate can design a scalable, secure, end-to-end real-time fraud pipeline across AWS services, with cross-account/data-sharing constraints and privacy controls.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven ingestion and fan-out
- Real-time scoring with SageMaker endpoints
- Data retention and lifecycle in S3
- Compact indexing in DynamoDB
- Remediation workflow via SQS
- Idempotency, DLQ, and schema evolution
- Privacy: masking, encryption at rest/in transit
- Multi-region replication and cross-account access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-CDK-like snippet (not executable)
const stream = new KinesisStream(...);
const processor = new SageMakerEndpoint(...);
stream.onRecord(async (r) => {
  const score = await processor.invoke(r);
  await storeRaw(r); // S3
  await upsertIndex(r.eventId, r.userHash, r.merchantId, score, scoreLabel(score));
  if (score > THRESHOLD) publishRemediation(r);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution for the incoming events?
- What are failure modes and how would you mitigate them across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design an end-to-end, real-time content workflow using SageMaker endpoints, with proper data security and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven AWS architectures
- S3 triggers, Lambda orchestration
- SageMaker endpoints for classification and NLP
- Data catalogs (DynamoDB), storage (S3)
- IAM, encryption, auditing, cost optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// example: invoking SageMaker endpoint from Lambda (Node.js)
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const runtime = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function classify(text, endpoint){
  const res = await runtime.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName: endpoint, ContentType:'text/plain', Body: text}).promise();
  return JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotency and exactly-once processing for duplicate PUT events?
- How would you monitor latency and error rates, and what would you do to reduce cold start impacts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with Rekognition Video, including asynchronous results and integration with DynamoDB and S3. It also exercises privacy, error handling, and cost trade-offs in a beginner-friendly AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Rekognition Video asynchronous APIs (moderation, labels)
- SNS/SQS notifications and S3 event triggers
- Lambda orchestration and retries
- DynamoDB upsert patterns and compact metadata design
- IAM least privilege and KMS for encryption
- Privacy, retention, and cost considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// process Rekognition output and update DynamoDB
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
async function upsertVideoIndex(videoId, s3Key, labels, status){
  const item = { videoId, s3Key, labelList: labels, moderationStatus: status, timestamp: Date.now() };
  await ddb.put({TableName:'VideoIndex', Item:item}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 1000+ concurrent videos and handle partial failures?
- What changes would you make to support different video formats and longer durations while controlling costs?
